From fba71080e2ec9e75fa9f289ca463da10926627e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:42:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: support 64-bit buffer atomics

Metal has no 64-bit atomic that returns a value: metal_atomic's
_valid_fetch_other_type and _valid_non_fetch_type admit int, uint and float
only, so there is no 64-bit fetch min/max, load, store, exchange or
compare-exchange. __HAVE_ATOMIC_ULONG_MIN_MAX__ (set by the GPU compiler on
GPUs that have it) does add a *void* atomic_min_explicit/atomic_max_explicit
for device atomic_ulong, and nothing else: no signed atomic_long exists, and
_valid_max_type admits "device ulong*" alone, so there is no threadgroup 64-bit
atomic at all.

Advertise VK_KHR_shader_atomic_int64 (shaderBufferInt64Atomics) and lower
OpAtomicUMax/OpAtomicUMin on a 64-bit device pointer to the void form, preceded
by a read of the cell when the result is used. The effect on memory is exactly
Vulkan's; the returned value is the cell's contents read just before the
operation, which differs from OpAtomicUMax's exact original value when another
invocation wins the race in between. Without the lowering those pipelines do
not compile at all. shaderSharedInt64Atomics stays false.

Tested with host/moltenvk/repro/atomic64.c: 32768 invocations racing on 64-bit
min/max with the CPU recomputing every expectation, the fetch results checked
against the values that were actually written, and the uncontended fetch result
checked exactly.

Generated-by: LLM
